7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,3,3,3,7,9,4,9,3,3,3,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,3,3,3,4,3,3,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**What the grid is made of.** Every input has three parts:\n- a vertical line of colour 4;\n- three 8 markers on the same row: one at each edge of the grid, and one where that row crosses the 4 line;\n- a multicoloured shape that sits against the left side of the 4 line.\n\nEverything to the right of the line is empty background.\n\n**What changes.** In each output, a copy of the shape appears on the right of the line. It is not a plain left–right mirror.\n\nIn pair 2, the input's top `1 1` sits at row 2, columns 5–6. The output's matching `1 1` appears at the bottom, row 12, columns 8–9. So the copy is flipped both vertically and horizontally. That is a 180° rotation, i.e. a point reflection.\n\nThe centre of that rotation is the middle 8 marker at (7,7): (y, x) maps to (2·7−y, 2·7−x). Checking (2,5)→(12,9) gives 1, and (6,2)→(8,12) gives 1. Pairs 1 and 3 work the same way about their middle 8 at (8,8). For example, in pair 3, (8,2)=5 maps to (8,14)=5, and (15,6)=5 maps to (1,10)=5.\n\n**Rule.**\n1. Find the 8 that does not touch a grid edge. That is the centre (cy, cx).\n2. Take every non-background cell left of column cx (the shape itself).\n3. Paint it at (2cy−y, 2cx−x) on the right side, skipping anything that lands outside the grid.\n4. The original stays as it is.\n\n**Test input.** The middle 8 is at (9,9) in a 19×19 grid. The shape in columns 2–8 rotates into columns 10–16, and every rotated cell stays inside the grid.\n\n**Teaching stages.** The code emits one canvas per colour of the shape as it is stamped, then the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    # centre: the 8 marker that is not on the grid border\n    cy = cx = None\n    for y, x in zip(*np.where(g == 8)):\n        if 0 < x < W - 1 and 0 < y < H - 1:\n            cy, cx = int(y), int(x)\n    out = g.copy()\n    if cy is None:\n        emit(out, \"No centre marker found; grid unchanged.\")\n        return out\n    line_col = g[cy, cx]\n    cells = [(y, x) for y in range(H) for x in range(cx)\n             if g[y, x] != bg]\n    colours = []\n    for y, x in cells:\n        if g[y, x] not in colours:\n            colours.append(int(g[y, x]))\n    for c in colours:\n        for y, x in cells:\n            if g[y, x] != c:\n                continue\n            ny, nx = 2 * cy - y, 2 * cx - x\n            if 0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W and out[ny, nx] == bg:\n                out[ny, nx] = c\n        emit(out, f\"Rotate the colour-{c} cells of the shape 180 degrees about the 8 marker at ({cy},{cx}) onto the right side of the line.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final: the shape is completed by its point-reflected copy.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is not a plain left–right mirror."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"That is a 180° rotation, i.e. a point reflection."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The centre of that rotation is the middle 8 marker at (7,7): (y, x) maps to (2·7−y, 2·7−x)."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Teaching stages.** The code emits one canvas per colour of the shape as it is stamped, then the final grid."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"a9a2f3856243c64a74731cebf4959082e446876071e3cd66942732217a4bd7c6","task_sha256":"785c31c36c4c7e9a426e5d89d12bf52c7c743987d2a96604b767b796dce2552a","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
